WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.500
A small company's app records card payments.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:12.053
Each payment is one row in a table: which shop was paid, the amount in cents, and whether it succeeded.

3
00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.835
Here are the ten from March.

4
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:16.155
Every shop wants to know what it took.

5
00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:19.195
So how do you turn many rows into one line per shop?

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.807
You could ask once per shop: the rows for shop 1, then shop 2, and so on.

7
00:00:23.807 --> 00:00:26.825
Eight trips, and you need the list of shops first.

8
00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:31.473
Or you could fetch every payment into your own program and add them up there.

9
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:33.021
With ten rows, fine.

10
00:00:33.021 --> 00:00:37.898
With a million, you'd ship a million rows out to work out 2,500 numbers.

11
00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:43.045
That's like posting a whole filing cabinet to someone, to ask for one total.

12
00:00:43.045 --> 00:00:46.093
Better to do the adding where the data lives.

13
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:52.104
The database starts with an aggregate function: one that takes many values and returns one.

14
00:00:52.104 --> 00:00:54.911
count counts rows, and sum adds up a column.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.146
Used alone, an aggregate folds every row it sees into one.

16
00:01:00.146 --> 00:01:10.239
Over all 37 payments in the course database, the succeeded ones fold into a single line: 30 payments, 720,455 cents.

17
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:12.166
Many rows in, one row out.

18
00:01:12.166 --> 00:01:16.164
But that's one total for everyone, and we want one per shop.

19
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:19.980
Picture a shoebox of paper receipts at the end of the day.

20
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.535
You'd sort them into one envelope per shop.

21
00:01:22.535 --> 00:01:27.348
Then you'd count the receipts in each envelope, and write the total on the front.

22
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:29.174
That's what GROUP BY does.

23
00:01:29.174 --> 00:01:30.562
Each receipt is a row.

24
00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:36.684
Each envelope is a group, one for each different shop number, and that number is the group's key.

25
00:01:36.684 --> 00:01:39.208
The total on the front is the aggregate.

26
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.734
Let's run it on March, for succeeded payments only.

27
00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:46.201
WHERE goes first, and it looks at one row at a time.

28
00:01:46.201 --> 00:01:52.601
Payment 15 failed, and payment 20 is still pending, so both are gone before any envelope exists.

29
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:54.144
Eight rows are left.

30
00:01:54.144 --> 00:01:56.190
How many lines will come out?

31
00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.319
Pause and guess.

32
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:00.906
Four.

33
00:02:00.906 --> 00:02:11.107
GROUP BY makes one envelope per shop it finds: shop 5's three receipts in one, shop 4's three in another, and one each for shops 1 and 7.

34
00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:15.038
Now each aggregate folds its envelope.

35
00:02:15.038 --> 00:02:19.843
count counts the receipts, and sum adds them up.

36
00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:23.648
Shop 5: three payments, 147,500 cents.

37
00:02:23.648 --> 00:02:25.850
Shop 4: three, 56,997.

38
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.653
Shop 1 and shop 7: one each.

39
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.277
Now a tempting idea.

40
00:02:30.277 --> 00:02:33.340
Why not show each shop's amount on its line too?

41
00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.489
For shops 1 and 7, there's only one amount, so it looks harmless.

42
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.780
But shop 5's line stands for three receipts, with three amounts.

43
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.565
Which one goes on the front?

44
00:02:43.565 --> 00:02:45.668
PostgreSQL won't play favourites.

45
00:02:45.668 --> 00:02:52.489
It stops with an error: the column must appear in the GROUP BY clause, or be used in an aggregate function.

46
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:55.650
That's the rule that trips up everyone at first.

47
00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.944
After grouping, one line stands for a whole envelope.

48
00:02:58.944 --> 00:03:05.657
So a column can appear there only if it has one value per envelope: the key you grouped by, or an aggregate.

49
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:06.822
One more limit.

50
00:03:06.822 --> 00:03:11.636
WHERE ran before the envelopes existed, so it can only judge single rows.

51
00:03:11.636 --> 00:03:18.955
To drop whole envelopes, say shops that took 100.00 or less, you need a second filter that runs after the fold.

52
00:03:18.955 --> 00:03:21.988
It's called HAVING, and it's the next section.

53
00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:23.534
So, one line per shop?

54
00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:27.992
Sort the rows into groups by a key, then fold each group into one row.

55
00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:30.793
And the adding happens where the data lives.

56
00:03:31.233 --> 00:03:32.355
One quick check.

57
00:03:32.355 --> 00:03:36.071
Shop 6, Paper Lantern Books, took no payments at all.

58
00:03:36.071 --> 00:03:38.245
Does it get a line with a zero?

59
00:03:38.245 --> 00:03:39.367
Pause and think.

60
00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:42.797
No.

61
00:03:42.797 --> 00:03:45.717
No receipts, no envelope, and no line.

62
00:03:45.717 --> 00:03:49.712
A group exists only if at least one row has its key.
